The diagonal $BD$ divides quadrilateral $ABCD$ into two triangles. Explain why its four interior angles add to $360^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.4);
\coordinate (B) at (2,3);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (1,0);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(D);
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[above] {$B$} (C) node[right] {$C$} (D) node[below] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Find the fourth interior angle $x$ of this quadrilateral. The square marks a right angle. The diagram is not to scale.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,2.5)--(1,2.5)--cycle;
\draw (3.7,2.5)--(3.7,2.2)--(4,2.2);
\path (0.65,0.35) node {$72^{\circ}$} (1.4,2.1) node {$108^{\circ}$} (3.6,0.4) node {$x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
